  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Determining trigonometric ratios in different quadrants</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>
The angle \(\theta={@theta@}^\circ\) is measured in standard position.</p>

<p>Use the unit circle to determine the exact values of:

1. \(\sin\theta=\) [[input:ans1]]
   [[validation:ans1]]

2. \(\cos\theta=\) [[input:ans2]]
   [[validation:ans2]]
</p>
<p>
Give your answers in exact form. Do not use decimal approximations.
</p>
<p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p><strong>Step 1: Identify the reference angle</strong></p>

<p>The given angle is \(\theta={@theta@}^\circ\). Its reference angle is \({@ref_angle@}^\circ\).</p>

<p>The reference angle is the acute angle between the terminal arm and the horizontal axis. It helps us determine the magnitudes of the trigonometric ratios.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Identify the quadrant</strong></p>

<p>Determine which quadrant contains the terminal arm of \(\theta\).</p>

<p>In Quadrant II, sine is positive and cosine is negative. In Quadrant III, both sine and cosine are negative.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Determine the sine</strong></p>

<p>Sine corresponds to the vertical coordinate of the point on the unit circle. Using the reference angle and the sign of sine in the relevant quadrant, we obtain</p>

<p>\(\sin\theta={@sin_theta@}.\)</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Determine the cosine</strong></p>

<p>Cosine corresponds to the horizontal coordinate of the point on the unit circle. Using the reference angle and the sign of cosine in the relevant quadrant, we obtain</p>

<p>\(\cos\theta={@cos_theta@}.\)</p>

<p><strong>Final answers</strong></p>

<p>\(\sin({@theta@}^\circ)={@sin_theta@}\)</p>

<p>\(\cos({@theta@}^\circ)={@cos_theta@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
